  {
    "episode_id": 65911,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/6/5/65911.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 49,
    "turn_text": "I think it's going to happen rather quickly. If you talk to our farmers, they want certainty. They want predictability. And when you lock in trade agreements, particularly with Mexico and Canada, that's going to allow purchase agreements to happen to flow of goods to go back and forth, particularly for corn and soybeans and pork and poultry. and sorghum and weed and all those things that we produce in Illinois, that will happen right now, well over 90% of the corn that Mexico imports comes from the United States. Breaking down these tariffs, breaking down these barriers between the countries is going to help for goods to continue to flow. And I think you'll see those purchase agreements come to fruition here very, very quickly as farmers begin to plant a little bit later in the spring. 40% of the products we grow produce or manufacture in Illinois go to Mexico or Canada. With this agreement in place, that is only going to go up and that's a positive for, again, jobs and economic opportunities in our area.",
