  {
    "episode_id": 65911,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/6/5/65911.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 55,
    "turn_text": "well we've had a lot of legislation equated on bringing down the high cost of prescription drugs and frankly we've been deficient as a congress on addressing that some You know, for a long time, we've left the drug companies, the pharmaceutical companies, try to self-regulate themselves. That clearly has not worked. No one, you know, companies and businesses don't like regulation, the government coming in. However, if you don't bring down the cost of prescription drugs and continue to gouge customers, whether they're seniors or people that need prescriptions, we have to step in and do some. The president supports that. Many Republicans support that and Democrats support that. And so I think you'll see legislation here in the next three months. Some of it through the Ways and Means Committee that I sit on that will go to the president that are going to hold these drug companies accountable, particularly more transparency so we know how much money they're making and where that money's going secondarily, holding them accountable for gouging people and taking advantage of people. And there's example after example after example where they continue to do that. So again, this is a bipartisan issue. We ought to work together Republicans and Democrats to do that. We need to bring relief to citizens when it comes to the high cost of prescription drugs.",
